It's Fun Run.

12
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.200
It's Fun Run Part 1.

13
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.220
That's right.

14
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:40.420
Because Fun Run was an hour-long episode, so once again, we're going to break it down into two parts.

15
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.760
Today is Fun Run Part 1, written by Greg Daniels, directed by Greg Daniels.

16
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.680
So much happens in Fun Run.

17
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.380
It needs to be two episodes.

18
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.560
It really does.

19
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:53.960
Oh, for sure.

20
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:54.900
For sure.

21
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.180
Here is a summary of just Part 1.

22
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:58.340
Yeah, let's hear it.

23
00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:58.980
Yeah, let's hear it.

24
00:00:58.980 --> 00:00:59.200
Okay.

25
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.620
Michael accidentally hits Meredith with his car and breaks her pelvis.

26
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.180
There's so much there already.

27
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:08.620
Already.

28
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.760
He gets the whole gang to visit her in the hospital and later discovers she may also have rabies.

29
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.040
Angela asks Dwight to give medicine to her cat, Sprinkles, while she visits Meredith in the hospital.

30
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:30.360
But when Dwight returns, he announces that Sprinkles is dead, and he puts Sprinkles in the freezer.

31
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.640
In the freezer!

32
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.860
Also, your television was on.

33
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.920
Yeah, my television was on, and Sprinkles is in the freezer.

34
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.500
Meanwhile, everyone wants to know if Jim and Pam are dating.

35
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.740
They insist they're not.

36
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.160
They say they're just friends.

37
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.260
But Kevin is not convinced.

38
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.320
Kevin really has a bee in his bonnet about it.

39
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:51.340
Yeah.

40
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.420
Why does Kevin care so much?

41
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.440
I don't know.

42
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.440
He's obsessed.

43
00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.540
Because this episode begins so dramatically,

44
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:06.560
we felt like we needed to speak with the woman at the center of this episode.

45
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:07.920
Absolutely.

46
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.560
We need to talk to Kate Flannery about getting hit by that car.

47
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:13.700
Yes.

48
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.740
So, guys, Kate chatted with us.

49
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.380
Sam, will you play the interview?

50
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.420
Kate Flannery is here today, you guys, with her tiny teacup.

51
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.340
Hi, Kate.

52
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.160
How are you?

53
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:29.660
I'm great.

54
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:30.460
How are you guys doing?

55
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.580
It's so nice to see you.

56
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.680
Wait, we have to get a picture of you with that teacup.

57
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:34.960
Cody, will you take a picture?

58
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.240
It's not tiny.

59
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.980
It's a regular-sized teacup.

60
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.080
What's tiny about that?

61
00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:40.960
I don't believe you.

62
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:41.620
It looks tiny.

63
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.460
It's regular.

64
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.160
Oh, my gosh.

65
00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.540
And you have a little cup and saucer.

66
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:47.360
I do.

67
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.220
Here's the thing, guys.

68
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.360
Kate Flannery is one of the classiest gals.

69
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.360
Her home is spectacular.

70
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.900
It is decorated like it's out of Architectural DigeSt.

71
00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.320
Every little thing is amazing.

72
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.100
And I am not surprised at all that you look so put together

73
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.620
and you're having your tea in a proper cup and saucer.

74
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.200
Oh, my gosh.

75
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:07.460
Oh, wait.

76
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.480
But now I got to turn my phone off because someone's calling me.

77
00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:10.820
She's getting an important phone call.

78
00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:11.480
Sorry.

79
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.160
Take it on air.

80
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:12.720
Take it on air.

81
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.340
Yeah, who is it?

82
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.200
Let's talk to them.

83
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.560
I think it was a potential spam, so I hung up on them.

84
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:16.980
I'm sorry.

85
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:17.500
Oh, dang it.

86
00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.660
I can't get locked in a timeshare just for you for the bit.

87
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.400
I'm sorry, you guys.

88
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:21.820
I can't.

89
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.580
It's so good to see you.

90
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:24.540
You don't understand.

91
00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.980
We spent every day together for nine years.

92
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:28.840
I know.

93
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.120
Oh, my God.

94
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:30.860
I know.

95
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.840
It was so much like a real office job in that respect.

96
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.100
It really was.

97
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.880
Just like hours at our desks.

98
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.700
And the fluorescent light.

99
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:39.140
Yes.

100
00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.040
I mean, that has a real effect on you.

101
00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:41.940
And the snacks.

102
00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.740
Come on.

103
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.260
That was the best spread.

104
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.160
Oh, God.

105
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.100
We had the best, best food ever.

106
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.840
It's all my dad ever talked about.

107
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.680
All the years that he visited, and I would pepper him with questions about,

108
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.780
so Steve Carell ate lunch with us today.

109
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:55.580
Wasn't that great?

110
00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.040
And he was like, did you see the steak they had?

111
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.560
It was always about the food.

112
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.500
Bill Kinsey.

113
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:02.740
Oh, Kate.

114
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.580
It's so good to see you.

115
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.080
And Jenna.

116
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.060
You guys.

117
00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:07.980
You guys.

118
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.480
Kate, we always like to start by asking people how they came to be on The Office.

119
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.540
I actually auditioned for the pilot for the part of Jan.

120
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.600
Allison Jones said, I think you're really right for this show, but I don't know if this

121
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.020
is your part.

122
00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:24.180
So clearly, they went a different way with Melora Harden.

123
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.140
And lo and behold, you guys shot the pilot.

124
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.940
I remember that summer, I was doing a sketch show once a week that was timed.

125
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.120
It was a two-minute sketch show.

126
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.140
So literally, at two minutes, the lights would go out.

127
00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:41.580
It was this live show in Hollywood at this theater on Melrose with one of the Scholar

128
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.200
brothers, Randy, was doing a sketch right before me.

129
00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.660
He's like, have you been watching that British show, The Office?

130
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.640
I'm so obsessed with it.

131
00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.300
He was like the first person to really tell me about it.

132
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.360
And I was like, yeah, I've seen a little bit.

133
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.820
He goes, you got to watch the whole thing.

134
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.880
So I kind of got obsessed.

135
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.740
And then come September, late August, early September, I had an audition.

136
00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.560
I couldn't make the first one because I was in Chicago with my aunt.

137
00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:01.620
And then I just came.

138
00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.040
I figured the part was going to get taken.

139
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.320
And they had another session a few days later.

140
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.820
And I went in and I read.

141
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.240
And I remember Alison Jones said, no makeup.

142
00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.700
And usually in LA, that means mascara, some foundation.

143
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.920
It means something.

144
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.240
It never really means no makeup, right?

145
00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.560
And they were like, literally, she literally had a box of tissues.

146
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.160
She's like, no makeup.

147
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.620
Wipe it off.

148
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.860
Get off, right?

149
00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.520
Anyway, and then I just remember there were a few actresses that I knew from Chicago that

150
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:33.800
were there.

151
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.140
And I felt like they booked everything.

152
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.440
I'm like, I'm never going to get this.

153
00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:41.820
But I just remember that room was Ken Quapas and Greg and Alison Jones.

154
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:48.760
And I'm sure Paul Lieberstein, I'm sure all the producers were there, the writer producers.

155
00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.440
It was like the most unique experience I think I'd ever had in an audition.

156
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.380
And then when I came out, I got home.

157
00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.200
And then Alison told me to go to Gower and do it again, do it one more time.

158
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.140
And do less, less, less, do less.

159
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.520
And then by the time I got home from there, I found out that I got the part.

160
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:06.740
What?

161
00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.400
You booked it on the drive home.

162
00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:08.720
Isn't that weird?

163
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:09.620
Girl, way to go.

164
00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.600
And I didn't have a cell phone, so I didn't know till I got home.

165
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:12.780
That's every actor's dream.

166
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.980
You didn't have a cell phone, so you didn't know.

167
00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.620
Those were the days, man.

168
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:17.860
Like, you know what?

169
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:19.680
They couldn't get a hold of you.

170
00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.260
You found out at home.

171
00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.060
I was playing hard to get on the drive home.

172
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:24.560
Wow.

173
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.880
Well, Kate, I was so excited because when you walked on the set that day, Kate and I

174
00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.680
had been doing an improv show called Bitch Planet.

175
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:33.860
All chick improv.

176
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.160
All chick improv.

177
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.500
And we'd been doing that for years.

178
00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.680
And then all of a sudden, my good friend was going to be sitting, like, right behind

179
00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:43.540
me for years.

180
00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.700
That was just amazing.

181
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:45.820
Who knew?

182
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.560
When four people were in our audience on a Sunday night for our show.

183
00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:50.500
At 10 o'clock.

184
00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.200
Kate, do you remember what your audition scene was?

185
00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:54.480
Yeah.

186
00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.400
It was from the Alliance.

187
00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:04.180
It was the whole reaction to the surprise party and Michael reading the birthday card

188
00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.320
and insulting Meredith like crazy.

189
00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:07.140
Yes.

190
00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:07.800
Amazing.

191
00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:08.960
Yeah.

192
00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:09.660
It was so awesome.

193
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:10.500
So awesome.

194
00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.160
So awkward.

195
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:11.900
So awful.

196
00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.680
So awful.

197
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.320
Happy Bird Day.

198
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.500
Happy Bird Day.

199
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:22.660
Well, Kate, we've talked a lot on this show about how once we were filming in that bullpen,

200
00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.940
we were all in the scene.

201
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:27.220
And so we were all each other's background.

202
00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:28.720
And that's very unique.

203
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.560
It's not like that on a lot of shows, right?

204
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.840
So we've sort of talked about the things we did in the background.

205
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.920
We talked about how Phyllis looked at gates.

206
00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.040
Remember, she looked at gates for her house for years.

207
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.420
I remember they told us, they told me when I booked the job that I was supposed to bring

208
00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.760
paperwork because they really wanted us to be looking like we were working.

209
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.480
And I loved that suggestion.

210
00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:55.060
I thought it was so, like, it's so method because then no one is suddenly acting like they're

211
00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:55.340
working.

212
00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:59.400
They're actually working and the camera catches them, which makes so much more sense.

213
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.860
So I actually brought my taxes the first season.

214
00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:07.160
I was going to say, Kate, I have a memory of you opening your mail and, like, having-

215
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:07.600
Oh, yeah, I did.

216
00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.260
I did.

217
00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.040
I brought a bag of stuff.

218
00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.360
And I think I also, like, snuck reading a book, which is kind of like a thing that people

219
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.620
do in an office anyway sometimes, you know?

220
00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:16.980
Well-

221
00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.040
Especially Meredith.

222
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:19.100
Well, I don't know.

223
00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.460
Does Meredith read a lot?

224
00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:21.340
Maybe not.

225
00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.820
I got into solitaire, clearly.

226
00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.320
That was, like, my main background choice for so much of it.

227
00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:30.880
But that was so much because the back of my head was in so much of it that my screen was

228
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.460
caught all the time on my computer.

229
00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.560
So I would just, rather than, like, go to some file at the last minute, I would just dump

230
00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.840
into solitaire because I knew I could just get right back into it.

231
00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.740
And I thought it was a funny thing for Meredith to be multitasking all the time with a game.

232
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:44.380
So stupid.

233
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:46.500
Well, you became really, really good at it.

234
00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:52.420
Actually, Creed and I would have many, many a competition, and I whooped his ass.

235
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:53.660
You did?

236
00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:59.160
I am still sort of hung up on the fact that they told you to come to set and bring paperwork.

237
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.660
Jenna, did they tell you that?

238
00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:01.220
They didn't tell you that?

239
00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:02.540
No.

240
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.080
You know, I don't remember that specifically, but I did bring some.

241
00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:10.480
I used to write down my grocery list a lot.

242
00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.540
I was constantly, or just general to-do lists I would do.

243
00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:18.280
I would journal, you know, but with a paper and pen.

244
00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:19.140
So funny.

245
00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.540
Now, Jenna, you and I had a little system that we created.

246
00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:23.560
Do you remember?

247
00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:24.320
You remember this one.

248
00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:24.740
You have to.

249
00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:27.640
It's one of my fondest memories of the show.

250
00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:27.860
What?

251
00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:28.220
What?

252
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:28.500
What?

253
00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:34.280
We had specific boxes of other people's real-life paperwork.

254
00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:35.320
Yes.

255
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:35.370
Yes.

256
00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.440
Phil Shea bought, like, medical records.

257
00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:39.760
It was bizarre.

258
00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:40.660
And these-

259
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:40.860
Yeah.

260
00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:41.070
...

261
00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:41.620
receipts.

262
00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:48.520
And I had this whole system where I was stapling, and I had a whole, like, certain things that

